 "caveats": [
  "Section 6.11 says propagating the band alone under-predicts the spread by an order of magnitude. Freezing the arm and propagating the tie resultant alone gives 0.94 to 1.02 pp against 2.85 to 3.31 full, a factor of 2.8 to 3.5, not ten. Only the axial-only band path, 0.15 pp, is an order of magnitude below, so the sentence is right about the instrument this study recommends and wrong about the band resultant.",
  "The compression centroid is far more robust than the reaction arm: it moves 9 mm across a structurally different forward model against the 100 mm the reaction arm is wrong by if the plate centre is assumed. The referee is right that the sensitivity is comparable and right that it is unquantified; the resulting budget line is nonetheless small, and the honest report is that the paper survives the objection.",
  "The three model-form bounds are all self-referential to one degree or another: both forward models are written in one repository by one author on identical geometry with the same steel law, so 9 mm is a lower bound on what a genuinely independent code would disagree by.",
  "delta = 3.5 mm sits at the limit point of the reference solver, so dyC/dlambda there is 233 mm per unit lambda against 59 mm on the rising branch. The load-error path through y_C is quoted as a range for that reason and should not be read as a single number.",
  "y_C is exactly independent of the trial parameter, verified to 0 mm over the trial grid, so displacing it is a pure lever-arm perturbation and does not contaminate the tie term."
 ],
